"Because Microsoft manages Cloud Shell, you always have access to the most recent versions of the Azure CLI and PowerShell modules right from any browser. You don't have to worry about keeping modules up to date." (https://learn.microsoft.com/en-ca/training/modules/intro-to-azure-cloud-shell/2-what-is-azure-cloud-shell)

The issue with the cloud shell updating modules is that it is hard to find which exact module version your application is running. A while back I was working on this relatively old application that was hosted in an Azure web app. The Azure CLI had been keeping modules up to date and yet sometimes packages get decommissioned. When I got the app to work on, a normal install of modules did not load my local code with the same packages that are used in the Azure shell... it loaded from the old projects package lock that included unsupported modules and essentially an unsupported app. In the cloud shell I tried to query which version of each required package either the front end or back end was using and it was extremely difficult and frustrating.
